\section*{Introduction to Unsupervised Learning}

This computer assignment will look into two to three widespread algorithms for unsupervised learning.

The dataset \texttt{iris} contain data on petal and sepal length and width for three different species of iris flowers. In addition, we will also use the \texttt{faithful} data on the Old Faithful Geyser in Yellowstone. The data consists of the eruption time (in minutes) and the waiting time (in minutes) until the next eruption. Finally we will also use the \texttt{mixture\_data} from Table 8.1 in  \citet{hastie2009elements}.

To access the data, just use:

<<echo=TRUE, eval=TRUE>>=
data("iris")
data("faithful")
library(uuml)
data("mixture_data")
@

For a more indepth description of the datasets, just run \texttt{?iris}, \texttt{?faithful}, or \texttt{?mixture\_data} in R.

\section{The k-means Algorithm}

We are now going to build a k-means algorithm from scratch.

\emph{Data convention:} Keep cluster labels in \texttt{C}, separate from the measurement columns in \texttt{X}. For the iris experiment, \texttt{X} is $150\times 2$, \texttt{C} has length 150, and \texttt{m} is $3\times 2$.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (1p) Visualize \texttt{iris} as a scatterplot of \texttt{Sepal.Width} and \texttt{Petal.Width}. Show the three species (the \texttt{Species} variable) using different colors. \emph{Hint!} Use \texttt{ggplot2} and \href{https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_point.html}{geom\_point}.
\item (1p) Visualize (for yourself) the \texttt{faithful} data and manually allocate each data point to two different clusters of your choosing based on the \texttt{eruptions} and/or \texttt{waiting} variables.
\item (1p) Now, visualize the \texttt{faithful} dataset as a scatterplot by \texttt{eruptions} and \texttt{waiting}. Show your manually defined clusters in the Figure using different colors.
\item (2p) Implement \texttt{compute\_cluster\_means(X, C)} following Step 1 of Algorithm 14.1 in \citet{hastie2009elements}. The inputs are an $n\times p$ measurement matrix \texttt{X} and a length-$n$ vector \texttt{C} of cluster labels $1,\ldots,K$. Return a $K\times p$ matrix \texttt{m}, with row $k$ containing the means for cluster $k$. Do not include a cluster-ID column.
% Keep the first cluster-means example together.
\enlargethispage{3\baselineskip}
<<eval = FALSE>>=
set.seed(4711)
X <- as.matrix(faithful)
C <- sample(1:3, nrow(X), replace = TRUE)
m <- compute_cluster_means(X, C)
m

##      eruptions  waiting
## [1,]  3.416354 70.57576
## [2,]  3.571106 71.44706
## [3,]  3.487659 70.72727
@
\item (2p) Next, implement the second step in Algorithm 14.1 of \citet{hastie2009elements} and call the function \texttt{compute\_cluster\_encoding(X, m)}. The function should take an $n\times p$ measurement matrix \texttt{X} and a $K\times p$ matrix \texttt{m} with one cluster mean per row. Return a length-$n$ vector of cluster labels $1,\ldots,K$.
<<eval=FALSE>>=
C <- compute_cluster_encoding(X, m)
C[1:10]
## [1] 2 1 2 1 2 1 2 2 1 2
@
\item (2p) Combine these functions into \texttt{k\_means(X,K)}, taking an $n\times p$ measurement matrix \texttt{X} and the number of clusters $K$. Randomly initialise the cluster labels and iterate until no assignments change. Return a list with \texttt{C} (length $n$) and \texttt{m} ($K\times p$). If any cluster is empty, either initially or after an update, restart the run with new random cluster assignments.
\item (2p) Implement a function called \texttt{k\_means\_W(X,C)} that computes the k-means within-point scatter (WPS) of Eq. 14.31 in \citet{hastie2009elements}.
<<eval = FALSE>>=
set.seed(4711)
X <- as.matrix(faithful)
C <- sample(1:3, nrow(X), replace = TRUE)
k_means_W(X, C)
## [1] 4601439
@
% Keep the comparison and its interpretation on one page.
\enlargethispage{3\baselineskip}
\item (1p) Run your \texttt{k\_means(X,K)} ten times per dataset with new random initialisations: \texttt{faithful} with $K=2$, and \texttt{iris} (\texttt{Sepal.Width}, \texttt{Petal.Width}) with $K=3$. Present each run's final WPS in a table for each dataset.

\emph{Hint!} Set \texttt{X} and \texttt{K} for each dataset. Set the seed once before the loop for reproducible results with different initialisations.
<<eval = FALSE>>=
set.seed(4711)
results <- vector("list", 10)
wps <- numeric(10)
for (r in seq_len(10)) {
  results[[r]] <- k_means(X, K)
  wps[r] <- k_means_W(X, results[[r]]$C)
}
@
\item (1p) For each dataset, plot the runs with the smallest and largest WPS among your ten runs. Choose any tied runs. If the selected clusterings are identical, one plot is sufficient; state this. Compare WPS only within each dataset.

\item (2p) How much did the final WPS vary between initialisations? What does this suggest about sensitivity to initialisation for each dataset?

\emph{Note!} Small or zero differences are valid results. You are not expected to obtain a particular gap between the smallest and largest WPS.
\end{enumerate}

\newpage

\section{Probabilistic PCA}

Probabilistic PCA (pPCA) describes noisy observations using fewer latent coordinates:
\[
\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{b}+\mathbf{W}\mathbf{h}+\boldsymbol{\varepsilon},
\qquad \mathbf{h}\sim N(0,I_K),\quad
\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}\sim N(0,\sigma^2 I_D),
\]
where the latent coordinates and noise are independent. This model lets us simulate observations around a lower-dimensional line or plane. See Section 13.1 in \citet{Goodfellow-et-al-2016}.

\examplebox{Generating a pPCA observation}{
With $\mathbf{W}=(-1,3)^T$, $\mathbf{b}=(0.5,2)^T$, $h=1$, and no noise, $\mathbf{x}=(-0.5,5)^T$. Changing $h$ moves the point along the line through $\mathbf{b}$ in direction $\mathbf{W}$. Adding noise scatters observations around this line.
}

\begin{enumerate}
\item (2p) Implement \texttt{pPCA(W, b, sigma2)} to return one simulated observation as a vector of length $D$. Here \texttt{W} is $D\times K$, \texttt{b} has length $D$, and \texttt{sigma2} is the noise variance. \emph{Hint!} Use \texttt{sqrt(sigma2)} as the noise standard deviation.
\item (1p) Simulate 300 observations with $\mathbf{W}=(-1,3)^T$, $\mathbf{b}=(0.5,2)^T$, and $\sigma^2=1$. Run \texttt{set.seed(4711)} before simulating. Store this baseline as \texttt{X\_base}, a $300\times 2$ matrix with one observation per row, and plot it as in Figure \ref{pPCA}.
\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.55\textwidth]{figs/pPCA.png}
\caption{Data from a pPCA model with $\mathbf{W} = (-1, 3)^T$, $\mathbf{b} = (0.5, 2)^T$ and $\sigma^2 = 1$}
\label{pPCA}
\end{figure}
\item (1p) Describe the different parts of the model. What are the parameters? What are the latent variables?
\item (1p) \emph{Before simulating, predict} the effect of each of these separate changes to the baseline: $\mathbf{b}=(2.5,2)^T$; $\mathbf{W}=(3,1)^T$; and $\sigma^2=4$. For each change, reset the seed to 4711 and simulate 300 observations. Show the baseline and three settings in one figure with common axis limits. Afterwards, briefly explain whether the results match your predictions.
\item (1p) Run PCA on the baseline data. Estimate the mean and the loading vector, accounting for the known baseline noise variance:
<<eval = FALSE>>=
sigma2 <- 1
pr <- prcomp(X_base, center = TRUE, scale. = FALSE)
b_hat <- pr$center
W_hat <- sqrt(max(pr$sdev[1]^2 - sigma2, 0)) * pr$rotation[, 1]
@
\emph{Why subtract the noise variance?} For one latent coordinate, $\operatorname{Cov}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{W}\mathbf{W}^T+\sigma^2 I$, so the leading eigenvalue is $\|\mathbf{W}\|^2+\sigma^2$. Centering removes the translation by $\mathbf{b}$; changing only $\mathbf{b}$ leaves centered PCA unchanged.
\item (1p) Compare \texttt{b\_hat} and \texttt{W\_hat} with the generating parameters. Explain how the results connect pPCA to classical PCA. Allow for sampling variation and an arbitrary sign of the principal direction.
\item (2p) Finally, simulate values $\mathbf{x}$ of dimension five from a probabilistic PCA model for a given choice of parameters. The latent variables $\mathbf{h}$ should be of dimension two, i.e. $\mathbf{h} \in \mathbb{R}^{2 \times 1}$ for each simulated observation. You are free to choose the parameter values $\mathbf{W}$, $\mathbf{b}$ and $\sigma^2$ yourself, but the resulting distribution should have at least one negative and one positive correlation between the $X$s. Visualize the variables using a pair-wise scatterplot using \texttt{pairs(X)} in R.
\end{enumerate}

\newpage

\section{* The EM-algorithm for soft clustering: Univariate Two-Component Normal Mixture}
\label{uni_two_comp}

\input{distinction_task.tex}

In the next step, we will implement the EM algorithm conceptually similar to k-means clustering. We are going to implement the EM by testing at the mixture data of \citet{hastie2009elements}. This dataset has been added to the course R package.

<<>>=
library(uuml)
data("mixture_data")
theta0 <- list(mu_1 = 4.12, mu_2 = 0.94, sigma_1 = 2, sigma_2 = 2, pi = 0.5)
@

\emph{Conventions:} Throughout this section, \texttt{theta} contains \texttt{mu\_1}, \texttt{mu\_2}, \texttt{sigma\_1}, \texttt{sigma\_2}, and \texttt{pi}. The sigmas are standard deviations. \texttt{pi} is the mixing weight for component 2, and \texttt{gamma[i]} is the posterior probability that observation $i$ belongs to component 2, given its value and the current parameters.

\begin{enumerate}
\item (2p) Simulate data from a univariate mixture model by implementing a mixture model as in Eq. 8.36 in \citet{hastie2009elements}. That is, create a hiearchical simulation that first sample $\delta$ for each observation, and then sample $Y_i$ from the relevant component. The function should be called \texttt{r\_uni\_two\_comp(n, theta)} take have the arguments \texttt{n} (the number of observations to simulate) (the number of simulated observations) and \texttt{theta}, an R list with with values \texttt{mu\_1}, \texttt{mu\_2}, \texttt{sigma\_1}, \texttt{sigma\_2}, and \texttt{pi}.
\item (1p) Simulate 200 observations using $\mu_1=-2$, $\mu_2=1.5$, $\sigma_1=2$, $\sigma_2=1$ and $\pi=0.3$. Visualize observations drawn in a histogram.
\item (2p) Implement a function \texttt{d\_uni\_two\_comp(x, theta)} that computes the density value for a given set of parameters \texttt{theta} for values of \texttt{x}. \emph{Hint!} The function \texttt{dnorm} might be handy.
\item (1p) Visualize the density for the mixture model above over the interval -6 to 6 (by steps of 0.01).
\item (1p) Visualize the \texttt{eruptions} variable in the \texttt{faithful} dataset using a histogram.
\item (1p) Manually choose values for $\mu_1$, $\mu_2$, $\sigma_1$, $\sigma_2$ and $\pi$ that gives a density you think is a good fit for the \texttt{eruptions} variable.
\item (2p) Implement \texttt{e\_uni\_two\_comp(X, theta)}. Here \texttt{X} contains $n$ univariate observations in an $n\times 1$ matrix. Return a vector \texttt{gamma} of length $n$, with one responsibility for component 2 per observation. This function should implement the expectation step in Algorithm 8.1 in \citet{hastie2009elements}. For definition of $\phi$ and $\theta$, see \citet[][, Section 8.5.1]{hastie2009elements}. Subscript in Algorithm 8.1, e.g. in $\theta_2$, indicates the component/cluster.
<<eval = FALSE>>=
gamma <- e_uni_two_comp(mixture_data, theta0)
head(gamma)
## [1]  0.9106339 0.8716861 0.7797225 0.6645640 0.6484311 0.5178799
@
\emph{Note!} Gamma values for component 1 is just 1 - \texttt{gamma}.
\item (1p) An observation has $\gamma_i = 0.6$. What weights does it receive for the two components in the M-step? Explain how this differs from a hard cluster assignment.
\item (2p) Implement a function called \texttt{max\_uni\_two\_comp(X, gamma)} that returns a list with parameters \texttt{mu\_1}, \texttt{mu\_2}, \texttt{sigma\_1}, \texttt{sigma\_2} and \texttt{pi}. This function should implement the maximization step in algorithm 8.1 in \citet{hastie2009elements}.
<<eval = FALSE>>=
theta <- max_uni_two_comp(mixture_data, gamma)
theta
## $mu_1
## [1] 3.842941
##
## $mu_2
## [1] 1.450413
##
## $sigma_1
## [1] 1.700666
##
## $sigma_2
## [1] 1.47168
##
## $pi
## [1] 0.4883709
##
@
\item (1p) Implement the log-likelihood of the model as \texttt{ll\_uni\_two\_comp(x, theta)}. \emph{Hint!} See Eq. 8.39 in \citet{hastie2009elements}.

\emph{Numerical hint:} Very small densities can underflow to zero. Use \texttt{dnorm(..., log = TRUE)} and add the log mixing weights before combining the components with the helper below. It computes $\log(\exp(a)+\exp(b))$ for each observation; sum these log-densities over observations.
<<eval = FALSE>>=
log_sum_exp <- function(a, b) {
  m <- pmax(a, b)
  value <- m + log(exp(a - m) + exp(b - m))
  ifelse(is.infinite(m), m, value)
}
@
<<eval = FALSE>>=
ll_uni_two_comp(mixture_data, theta0)
## -43.1055
@
\newpage
\item (2p) Combine the functions into \texttt{em\_uni\_two\_comp(X, theta\_0, iter)}. It takes an $n\times 1$ data matrix \texttt{X} and initial parameters \texttt{theta\_0}. Run \texttt{iter} iterations, printing the current log-likelihood. Return a list with the final parameters as \texttt{theta} and an $\texttt{iter}\times 5$ matrix \texttt{history}. Row $i$ contains the parameters after M-step $i$; use columns \texttt{mu\_1}, \texttt{mu\_2}, \texttt{sigma\_1}, \texttt{sigma\_2}, and \texttt{pi}.
<<eval = FALSE>>=
em3 <- em_uni_two_comp(mixture_data, theta0, iter = 3)
## Log Lik:  -41.53247
## Log Lik:  -41.11211
## Log Lik:  -40.48348
@
\item (1p) Test your algorithm on the \texttt{mixture\_data} for 20 iterations. Do you get the same results for $\hat{\pi}$ as in \citet[p. 275]{hastie2009elements}? Note that you might get slightly different results (at the second decimal place). Also, compare your log-likelihoods with Figure 8.6 in \citet{hastie2009elements}. Do you have a similar result? \emph{Hint!} There can be a slight different order depending on if you compute the log-likelihood value before or after the update of $\theta$.
<<eval = FALSE>>=
em20 <- em_uni_two_comp(mixture_data, theta0, iter = 20)
## Log Lik:  -41.53247
## ...
@
\item (1p) Run your EM algorithm on the \texttt{eruptions} variable of the \texttt{faithful} data and on the \texttt{Petal.Length} variable of the \texttt{iris} data until you judge it has converged. What are the estimated parameters for the two datasets and how do you assess convergence?
\item (1p) Visualize the density for the two datasets in two different figures using the parameters estimated with your EM algorithm. Also show histograms for the real data in the same Figures (i.e. so you show the data and the estimated density). \emph{Hint!} You should be able to use \texttt{d\_uni\_two\_comp(x, theta)}.
\end{enumerate}



